    "knowledge_point": "Finding Distributions of Squared Random Variables: One-to-One Transformations",
    "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n\nLet $Y=3{X}^{3}.$ The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $a$ | $0$ | $b$ | $24$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $c$ | $0.2$ | $d$ | $0.3$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $b+d?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=3{X}^{3}$ row to our table:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $24$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $24$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n\nTherefore, $b=3,$ $d=0.4,$ and $b+d=3.4.$",
